      "content": "The backgrounds and traits you've described for Marcus and Andre do suggest a tilt toward a gritty crime genre, primarily from their environments and inner worlds. Marcus's rough exterior and his tumultuous relationship with the law naturally point toward a gritty setting without needing much elaboration. His very presence evokes the kind of world where his dark streetwear is relevant and his complex relationship with the law suggests an urban underworld or a crime-ridden area. Andre's street-smart characteristics and his deep connection with the local underground scene are archetypes commonly found in crime procedurals, where his knowledge of the criminal grapevine would be invaluable, potentially driving plot development further into the murky underworld. It's less about making assumptions and more about recognizing that these traits and backgrounds have certain narrative predispositions as they often go hand-in-hand with the genre characteristics we're stereotypically familiar with. If you enjoyed the genre-specific resonance, it's a good sign you're writing to fulfill the expectations those traits bring. However, always remember that an author's intent can override these associations through unexpected storytelling.",
