      "content": "The characters don't necessarily have a \"predisposition\" one way or another; it's more about the implication of their backgrounds on the story they can tell. Kenji's tech-savvy nature and preference for dimly lit rooms might immediately evoke imagery of a character specializing in hacking or cyber crimes, given these traits align closely with the themes and settings of a tech-centric plot. Kenji might fit naturally in any setting that involves technology - tech dramas or even historical ones with a sci-fi twist, where his character could represent a contrast to more traditional roles.\n\nEleanor, on the other hand, with her refined accent and management of a massive estate, seems to fit specific world-class, high-society fiction, which includes drama but not always genre fiction like gangsters or cybercrimes. Her peaceful estate in Connecticut paints her as a character possibly embroiled in social issues or familial drama, less aligned with the gritty rawness of a crime procedural.\n\nFor Chloe, living in a high-end penthouse and attending charity galas paints a picture of elite social standing and involvement in high society for philanthropy or social climbing, which isn't intrinsically linked to the hard-hitting tropes in genre fiction.\n\nYour concern about leaning on racial or cultural shorthand",
